    id: "the-wise-elder", kind: "integrated",
    name: "The Wise Elder",
    highLines: ["Crystallized", "Existential", "Interpersonal"],
    lowLines: [],
    pattern: "Deep accumulated knowledge (crystallized intelligence) integrated with reflective, perspectival, existential judgment — knowledge fused with wisdom about how to use it in the messy realities of life.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Crystallized knowledge without wise-reasoning integration produces the 'expert who is often wrong but never in doubt': high certainty, poor perspective-taking, intolerance of ambiguity, and brittle judgment on ill-defined life problems. Intelligence alone does not confer the meta-cognitive humility (recognizing limits of one's knowledge, considering others' viewpoints, integrating change) that wisdom requires.",
    connectionCase: "Wisdom in the Baltes Berlin paradigm is expertise in the fundamental pragmatics of life — rich factual/procedural knowledge PLUS lifespan contextualism, value relativism, and management of uncertainty. Crucially, Grossmann and colleagues found that wise reasoning, not IQ, predicts wellbeing outcomes. Wisdom is developed through reflective dialogue, mentorship, and adopting a self-distanced/third-person perspective — i.e., it is partly trainable and socially cultivated.",
    growthMeasures: "Grossmann et al. (2013, 'A Route to Well-Being'): wise reasoning predicted greater life satisfaction, less negative affect, better social relationships, and lower mortality risk over time, whereas general intelligence did NOT predict these outcomes. Berlin studies show wisdom-related performance can be raised experimentally (e.g., cluing on others' perspectives, and self-distancing manipulations reliably increase wise-reasoning scores). Honest caveat: the wisdom-wellbeing link is measure-dependent and mixed — some studies find wise reasoning weakly or even negatively related to global life-satisfaction at the extreme high end, so effects are modest, not sweeping.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Grossmann, I., Na, J., Varnum, M. E. W., Kitayama, S., & Nisbett, R. E. (2013). A route to well-being: Intelligence versus wise reasoning. Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 142(3), 944-953.", finding: "Wise reasoning predicted well-being, better relationships, and lower mortality risk; IQ did not — but effects were modest and some wellbeing facets were unrelated; limitation: correlational, single cultural sample per study.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1037/a0029560" },
      { cite: "Baltes, P. B., & Staudinger, U. M. (2000). Wisdom: A metaheuristic (pragmatic) to orchestrate mind and virtue toward excellence. American Psychologist, 55(1), 122-136.", finding: "Defines wisdom as expert knowledge in life's fundamental pragmatics (5 criteria) that integrates knowledge with virtue; wisdom-related performance is rare and can be facilitated by dialogic/reflective conditions; limitation: vignette-based, expert-rated performance.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.55.1.122" },
      { cite: "Grossmann, I., & Kross, E. (2014). Exploring Solomon's paradox: Self-distancing eliminates the self-other asymmetry in wise reasoning about close relationships. Psychological Science, 25(8), 1571-1580.", finding: "Adopting a self-distanced (third-person) perspective raised wise-reasoning quality about one's own problems to the level seen for others' problems — wisdom is state-manipulable, not fixed; limitation: reasoning measured on vignettes, short-term.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797614535400" },
    ],
