    untreatedTrajectory: "Foley Nicpon, Allmon, Sieck & Stinson (2011), reviewing two decades of empirical work on twice-exceptionality, conclude that gifted students frequently carry a co-existing learning disability, ADHD, or ASD that goes unrecognized because high ability compensates until demands outstrip it, and that students with disabilities are more underrepresented in gifted programming than any other group. Silverman (1997) frames this as asynchronous development, where advanced reasoning masks lagging function and produces a child who looks merely 'average' on both dimensions. Honest limitation: this literature is largely observational and clinic-referred, without randomized designs, so it establishes association and identification gaps rather than clean causal outcomes.",
    connectionCase: "Foley Nicpon et al. (2011) argue that comprehensive, individualized assessment that measures strengths and deficits simultaneously (rather than a single composite score) is what surfaces these students, and recommend pairing a gifted plan with an IEP so the high line is developed while the low line is accommodated. The review notes, however, that evidence on which interventions actually work remains thin.",
