    name: "Practice Meets the Raw Material: Deliberate Practice + Domain Talent",
    highLines: ["Volitional", "Systematic"],
    lowLines: [],
    pattern: "Sustained, effortful, feedback-driven practice is what converts potential into expert performance — but the honest reading of the evidence is that practice works WITH pre-existing ability differences, not instead of them. The combination, not practice alone, produces elite performers.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "The 'anyone can be great with 10,000 hours' framing overstates the case: even among people who practice enormously, ability and other individual differences still separate the best from the rest. Betting purely on hours ignores that most of the reliable variance in performance sits outside deliberate practice. Conversely, talent without disciplined practice rarely reaches expert levels at all.",
    connectionCase: "Ericsson, Krampe & Tesch-Romer (1993) documented that elite violinists had accumulated far more deliberate practice (the 'best' averaging 10,000+ hours by age 20), establishing practice as central to expertise. Macnamara, Hambrick & Oswald's (2014) meta-analysis then quantified its real ceiling: deliberate practice matters, but explains only part of performance variance, leaving substantial room for talent, starting age, and other factors.",
    growthMeasures: "Macnamara et al. (2014): deliberate practice explained 26% of variance in games, 21% in music, 18% in sports, 4% in education, <1% in professions; correcting for measurement error, ~19% of reliable variance overall — meaning ~81% is attributable to other factors. Ericsson et al. (1993) relied on retrospective practice estimates, a known methodological limitation.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Ericsson, K. A., Krampe, R. T., & Tesch-Romer, C. (1993). The Role of Deliberate Practice in the Acquisition of Expert Performance. Psychological Review, 100(3), 363-406.", finding: "Accumulated deliberate practice tracked violinist skill level; the 'best' group averaged 10,000+ hours by age 20. Limitation: retrospective self-estimates of practice and a small sample; the strong 'practice explains almost everything' claim was later tempered.", kind: "scholar", ref: "https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Ericsson+Krampe+Tesch-Romer+1993+deliberate+practice+expert+performance" },
      { cite: "Macnamara, B. N., Hambrick, D. Z., & Oswald, F. L. (2014). Deliberate Practice and Performance in Music, Games, Sports, Education, and Professions: A Meta-Analysis. Psychological Science, 25(8), 1608-1618.", finding: "Deliberate practice explained a modest and domain-varying share of performance variance (26% games to <1% professions), refuting the view that practice is nearly all that matters. Limitation: relies on the primary studies' often retrospective practice measures.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797614535810" },
    ],
