    name: "Grouping same-line peers together (the honest meta-analytic picture)",
    highLines: ["Logical", "Mathematical", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    lowLines: [],
    pattern: "Placing high-ability students with same-ability peers (within-class, cross-grade, or special gifted grouping) versus leaving them dispersed in mixed-ability classes affects academic growth.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "The largest second-order meta-analysis of the question found that simply keeping high-ability students in mixed, between-class settings produced essentially no benefit (g = 0.04-0.06, effectively null), i.e. dispersion among non-peers neither helps nor is a growth engine (Steenbergen-Hu, Makel & Olszewski-Kubilius, 2016).",
    connectionCase: "The same meta-analysis found that when students were actually clustered with same-line peers, growth appeared: within-class grouping g = 0.19-0.30, cross-grade subject grouping g = 0.26, and special grouping for the gifted g = 0.37 (Steenbergen-Hu, Makel & Olszewski-Kubilius, 2016). Acceleration into a more advanced peer group yielded g = 0.42.",
    growthMeasures: "Effects are real but modest-to-moderate, not dramatic: special gifted grouping g = 0.37 (~a third of a standard deviation); between-class grouping is a null result. The authors explicitly caution that the form of grouping matters far more than grouping per se, and effects are heterogeneous across studies.",
