    pattern: "Strong aesthetic and creative-production capacity paired with weak financial self-management, so intrinsic pull toward the work outruns the ability to make it pay or to stabilize income.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Labor-economics research finds this is a structural pattern, not just a personal failing. Alper & Wassall (2006), analyzing six decades of U.S. census data, found artists earn less, face more unemployment, and hold multiple jobs more often than comparable workers, and that the earnings gap persisted even among full-time year-round workers and did not shrink over 60 years. The dominant economic explanation is a compensating-differential / 'work-preference' trade-off (Menger): artists accept low and unstable pay in exchange for the intrinsic rewards of creative work, and excess supply to the field keeps wages down. Honest limitation: this is observational population economics, not a psychometric claim that creative talent causes poor money management; the mechanism is largely market structure and self-selection, and many artists diversify income successfully.",
    connectionCase: "Naming the pattern as a known labor-market structure (rather than personal inadequacy) reframes it: the fix is financial scaffolding — multiple-jobholding by design, pricing, contracts, and cash-flow skill — layered onto the creative gift, plus peer networks that share business practices, which the research treats as ordinary risk-management for the occupation.",
