    pattern: "High humor output channeled into self-disparagement and deflection rather than self-understanding — being the butt of the joke to gain approval or to avoid dealing with a problem, with little inward reflection.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Humor is not uniformly protective; its style matters. Martin et al. (2003), developing the Humor Styles Questionnaire, found self-defeating humor correlated positively with depression and anxiety, while the two adaptive styles (affiliative, self-enhancing) correlated negatively — directly separating 'making others laugh at your own expense' from healthy humor. A large meta-analysis (Guo et al., 2020; 85 studies, N=27,562) confirmed affiliative and self-enhancing humor raise subjective well-being while aggressive and self-defeating humor damage it, with culture and age not moderating the effect. Honest limitations: these are cross-sectional correlations (self-defeating humor and low mood likely reinforce each other rather than one strictly causing the other), and the HSQ's construct validity has been critiqued; humor as such is not the problem — the deflecting, self-disparaging style is.",
