    name: "The Gifted Loner",
    highLines: ["Logical", "Existential"],
    lowLines: ["Interpersonal"],
    pattern: "Exceptional reasoning and abstract/existential depth combined with felt social disconnection — the 'too smart to fit in' experience. Note: the configuration is real for some individuals, but the popular assumption that giftedness generally causes social maladjustment is largely a myth.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "The evidence corrects the stereotype more than it confirms it. Longitudinal work (Lubinski & Benbow, 2006, Study of Mathematically Precocious Youth, 35-year follow-up, >5,000 participants) found intellectually precocious individuals generally do very well in life, with subjective well-being above general-population norms — i.e., high ability is NOT, on average, associated with worse social or emotional outcomes. Moderately gifted children are typically as well adjusted as peers. A qualification exists at the extreme tail: research on the profoundly gifted (Gross; the classic Hollingworth 'optimal-adjustment zone') suggests social difficulty and isolation rise mainly above roughly IQ 145-160, where finding true peers becomes genuinely rare. Honest bottom line: the loner-genius trope is unsupported as a general rule; the trade-off is real chiefly for the rare profoundly gifted and is driven by peer-availability and fit, not by intellect being inherently anti-social.",
