    untreatedTrajectory: "The intuitive story ('people who don't feel their bodies can't name their feelings') is only partly supported. Trevisan et al. (2019), a meta-analysis (66 samples, N≈7,146, Journal of Abnormal Psychology), found alexithymia had essentially NO significant association with objective interoceptive accuracy (heartbeat-detection performance) and only a small negative association with self-reported interoceptive sensibility/awareness. So low objective body-sensing does not straightforwardly equal emotional blindness. Interoception is itself multi-dimensional: Garfinkel et al. (2015) empirically dissociated interoceptive accuracy, sensibility, and metacognitive awareness, and low interoceptive metacognition/awareness — not raw accuracy — is what most reliably tracks difficulties in emotion and anxiety. Honest correction: a 'high logic, low body-sense' profile is real and can co-occur with poorer emotion regulation, but the mechanism runs through subjective interoceptive AWARENESS, not through failing an objective heartbeat test; conflating the two overstates the case.",
