    id: "life-terman-gifted-arc", kind: "archetype",
    name: "The High-IQ Child Grown Old",
    highLines: ["Logical", "Mathematical", "Linguistic", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    lowLines: ["Interpersonal", "Adaptive"],
    pattern: "A child identified early by a single towering measure (IQ 135+) and expected, on that basis alone, to become eminent. This arc traces what actually happens to such children across a full lifespan.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Terman's Genetic Studies of Genius (begun 1921; Terman & Oden 1959) followed ~1,528 high-IQ California children into old age and found that as adults they were healthier, better-educated, and more occupationally successful than average, BUT that high childhood IQ alone did NOT reliably produce eminence, and the group fared no better than the general population on divorce, alcoholism, or suicide (Kell & Lubinski, SAGE Encyclopedia summary 2018). HONEST LIMITATION: the sample was non-representative — nearly all white, urban, middle-class Californians hand-screened partly via teacher nomination, with no matched average-IQ control group, and Terman himself intervened in subjects' lives (writing recommendations, advising on college), contaminating outcomes (Warne 2020).",
    connectionCase: "Within Terman's own data, adult achievement separated less on IQ than on non-cognitive factors — persistence, self-confidence, family encouragement, and opportunity — so the children whose environments supplied support and direction converted raw ability into accomplishment while equally bright peers plateaued. The study is a caution as much as a promise: a single high line is a starting condition, not a destiny, and connection/scaffolding across the life course is what moves the arc.",
    growthMeasures: "Descriptive/correlational and non-experimental: the gifted group's occupational attainment sat well above the college-graduate average at midlife, yet within-group eminence was predicted by personality and environment rather than by IQ rank. No matched control group existed, so 'IQ caused these outcomes' cannot be claimed. Later re-analyses (e.g., Warne 2020) confirm most core patterns replicate while flagging the selection and interference problems.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Terman, L. M., & Oden, M. H. (1959). Genetic Studies of Genius, Vol. V: The Gifted Group at Mid-Life. Stanford University Press.", finding: "High-IQ children became above-average adults in health, education, and occupation but not uniformly eminent, and matched population rates on divorce/alcoholism/suicide. Limitation: no average-IQ control group; non-representative hand-screened California sample.", kind: "scholar", ref: "Terman Oden 1959 Genetic Studies of Genius Gifted Group at Mid-Life" },
      { cite: "Warne, R. T. (2020). 'Ten little-known facts about the Terman longitudinal study of the gifted.'", finding: "Documents that Terman meddled in subjects' lives and that the sample lacked a matched control, while noting most substantive findings still replicate. Limitation: a secondary commentary, not primary data.", kind: "scholar", ref: "Warne 2020 ten little-known facts Terman longitudinal study gifted" },
    ],
