    id: "life-smpy-precocity-arc", kind: "archetype",
    name: "The Precocious Reasoner at Maturity",
    highLines: ["Mathematical", "Spatial", "Logical"],
    lowLines: ["Adaptive"],
    pattern: "A pre-teen who scores in the top fraction of a percent on quantitative or verbal reasoning — profoundly asymmetric ability spotted before age 13. This arc traces whether that early spike predicts real adult creative output.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "The Study of Mathematically Precocious Youth (SMPY; Lubinski & Benbow 2006, 35-year report on 5,000+ participants; Kell, Lubinski & Benbow 2013, N=320 top-1-in-10,000 followed to age 38) found that ability measured before age 13 predicted, decades later, doctorates, STEM publications, patents, income, and tenure at research universities — a dose-response relationship where even within the top 1%, higher scores meant more creative accomplishment. Left un-accelerated, however, precocious students are frequently under-challenged in ordinary classrooms; SMPY's own thesis is that ability without matched educational 'dose' underdevelops.",
    connectionCase: "SMPY's practical finding is that educational acceleration and ability-matched opportunity change the arc: precocious youth given advanced coursework, mentorship, and early access to challenge produced disproportionately more later output. Spatial ability — routinely un-measured and un-served by schools — emerged as an added predictor of creative/technical accomplishment, implying that identifying and feeding the specific high line, not just general brightness, is what converts precocity into contribution.",
    growthMeasures: "By age 38 the top-1-in-10,000 cohort (N=320) had produced 392 refereed STEM publications, 128 creative written works, 800+ software/patent contributions, and $25M+ in grants (Kell, Lubinski & Benbow 2013). Correlational and observational — participants were talent-search self-selectors and often received acceleration, so ability and opportunity are entangled; the design shows prediction, not proof of a purely intrinsic effect.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Lubinski, D., & Benbow, C. P. (2006). Study of Mathematically Precocious Youth after 35 years. Perspectives on Psychological Science, 1(4), 316-345.", finding: "Pre-age-13 reasoning ability predicts adult educational/occupational attainment across 5,000+ participants; argues ability must be matched by educational dose. Limitation: talent-search self-selection; correlational.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-6916.2006.00019.x" },
      { cite: "Kell, H. J., Lubinski, D., & Benbow, C. P. (2013). Who Rises to the Top? Early Indicators. Psychological Science, 24(5), 648-659.", finding: "Top-1-in-10,000 youth followed to age 38 produced high rates of patents, STEM publications, and creative works. Limitation: no equivalently able un-identified comparison group; observational.", kind: "doi", ref: "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797612457784" },
    ],
