    untreatedTrajectory: "Peer-reviewed work increasingly shows local/traditional ecological knowledge (LEK/TEK) is accurate and sometimes catches signals scientific surveys miss. Fishers' LEK provided valid insights into how environmental change is affecting coastal fishing resources, complementing formal data (Nunes et al., Proceedings of the Royal Society B, 2025). In Pacific island communities, resource users detected long-term ecological change (e.g., seagrass expansion) that matched independent satellite evidence (Lauer & Aswani, work summarized in Indigenous Knowledge and Long-term Ecological Change, 2009). Limitation: LEK is context-specific, can be biased by shifting baselines and memory, and requires careful cross-validation — it complements rather than replaces instrumented data. The 'untreated' cost is societal, not clinical: this intelligence is systematically under-elicited and under-compensated.",
