    pattern: "The page's 'intelligence archetypes' presume a shared idea of what intelligence is. Cross-cultural research shows cultures value strikingly different abilities — and that Western academic intelligence can even trade off against locally valued competence.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Sternberg et al.'s (2001) Kenya study of rural children found that scores on tacit knowledge of indigenous herbal medicines were NEGATIVELY correlated with conventional academic-intelligence measures — children strong in the locally adaptive intelligence often scored lower on Western tests, undermining any single universal 'g'-ranking of people. Sternberg (2004) argues intelligence cannot be meaningfully assessed outside cultural context, so imposing one culture's conception mismeasures the others (though critics read the negative correlation as a schooling trade-off, not proof of separate intelligences).",
    connectionCase: "The stable cross-cultural finding is meta: every culture studied has a rich, valued conception of intelligence — Sternberg (2004) documents that these reliably extend beyond abstract analysis to practical and social/interpersonal competence (e.g., Taiwanese conceptions include knowing when to display ability). What holds universally is that intelligence is embedded in and adapted to a social context, not that any one test ranks it.",
