    pattern: "A lifelong neurodevelopmental pattern that went unrecognized until adulthood; diagnosis reframes decades of struggle as wiring rather than character failure.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Before naming, adults with undiagnosed ADHD typically internalize repeated failures as personal or moral defects, accruing self-blame, low self-esteem, and secondary anxiety/depression (Ginapp et al., 2022). In women especially, years of undiagnosed ADHD are associated with adverse mental-health and functional outcomes that the eventual diagnosis is described as reframing (Attoe & Climie, per the women's-ADHD literature; Nature Sci Rep 2025). Honest limit: the label is not purely liberating - qualitative reviews find diagnosis is 'bittersweet,' often triggering grief and anger over lost time, and some fear stigma or worry the label becomes a self-limiting excuse.",
    connectionCase: "Receiving the diagnosis functions as a 'lightbulb' reframe: participants describe relief, biographical re-interpretation, reduced self-blame, and access to treatment and accommodations (Ginapp et al., 2022; McGill, Jardim-Lalor & O'Connor, 2026). Many integrate ADHD into identity as carrying both strengths and difficulties rather than pure deficit.",
    growthMeasures: "Evidence is qualitative/thematic (syntheses of ~35 and dozens of studies), not effect-size-based. Consistent themes across studies: relief, self-understanding, and reduced self-blame, counterbalanced by grief over lost time and unmet need for post-diagnostic support.",
