    highLines: ["General"],
    lowLines: [],
    pattern: "The failure mode of non-identification: when one salient label so dominates perception that other real conditions are misattributed to it and never named.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "In diagnostic overshadowing, a person's intellectual-disability label is so salient that co-occurring mental-health conditions get falsely attributed to the disability and go untreated (Mason & Scior, 2004). Experimental work shows even trained psychologists' clinical impressions are biased downward by an ID label - fewer mental-health problems identified - delaying accurate care and raising the risk of further harm (bias-of-clinical-impressions study, 2025). Honest note: the concept illustrates that leaving patterns un-named has documented clinical costs, but it is a specific attention/attribution bias, not proof that every label helps.",
    connectionCase: "By naming diagnostic overshadowing itself, the field made an invisible attribution bias visible and correctable - the meta-lesson of the identification thesis: recognizing and labeling a pattern (even a bias) is what makes it addressable rather than perpetually misattributed (Mason & Scior, 2004).",
    growthMeasures: "Evidence is empirical/experimental on clinician judgment (vignette and impression studies) rather than patient-outcome effect sizes; consistent finding is systematic under-recognition of comorbid mental illness when an ID label is present.",
